= Distributed system — Technical =

== Model dimensions ==
Distributed-system models specify assumptions about:

* process failures;
* channel reliability;
* timing/synchrony;
* membership;
* adversarial behavior;
* stable storage.

Guarantees are meaningful only relative to these assumptions.

== Partial orders ==
Without a global clock, event ordering can be represented by happens-before relations.

Logical clocks and vector clocks encode causal/order information without requiring perfectly synchronized physical time.

== Consensus ==
Consensus asks nonfaulty participants to agree on a value while satisfying validity and termination conditions.

Impossibility and lower-bound results depend on the failure/timing model.

== Replicated state ==
Replication protocols trade among consistency, latency, availability, throughput and fault tolerance.

State-machine replication orders operations so replicas execute a common deterministic history.

